 "parts": [
  "In nineteen sixty-nine, the United States Army turned off one of the Niagara Falls. On purpose.",
  "It was the American Falls.",
  "The Corps had almost twenty-eight thousand tons of rock and earth dumped into the river.",
  "The dam was six hundred feet long.",
  "The water was sent over the Horseshoe Falls instead.",
  "On June twelfth, the dam was finished. The American Falls dried up.",
  "Why?",
  "Major rockfalls, in nineteen thirty-one and nineteen fifty-four, had piled broken rock at the base.",
  "The Army Corps wanted to study the cliff.",
  "Crews drilled rock cores and wet the stone with sprinklers.",
  "It stayed dry for more than five months.",
  "On November twenty-fifth, the dam came out.",
  "The water returned, and the falls went back to how they were.",
  "The rock pile stayed.",
  "The Corps concluded it was probably helping hold the cliff up.",
  "Later, a commission from both countries chose to leave it alone.",
  "Would you have left the rocks there?"
 ],
